##DESCRIPTION
##  Exam problem related to finding a point given cosine
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Trigonometry)
## DBchapter(Trigonometric functions)
## DBsection(Trigonometric functions of non-special angles)
## Date(9/11/2012)
## Institution(Mississippi College)
## Author(John Travis)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## KEYWORDS('trigonometry', 'angle', 'degree')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$x = random(-9, -5);
$r = random( 1, 3) -
    $x;    #  this will force r to be positive and larger in magnitude than x
$y = Compute("sqrt($r^2-($x)^2)")
    ;      #  y is positive since sine is given to be positive

#  Text

BEGIN_PGML
Suppose [``\cos(\alpha) = \frac{[$x]}{[$r]} ``] and [`\sin(\alpha) `] is positive.

If the point [` ([$x],y)`] is on the terminal side of the angle [`\alpha`] then (without simplifying any square roots) 
[`y = `][_]{$y}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The point will be on the circle [` x^2+y^2 = r^2 `] with [`x=[$x]`]. Also, since 
[``` \cos(\alpha) = \frac{x}{r} =  \frac{[$x]}{[$r]} ```] so [`x=[$x]`] and [`r=[$r]`]. 
Using the circle and solve for [`y`], which is positive because [`\sin(\alpha)>0`]. 
[```  y = \sqrt{r^2-x^2} = \sqrt{([$r])^2-([$x])^2} = \sqrt{[$r**2-$x**2]}```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
